// checkout-web's own C4 — what is INSIDE the element the fleet map binds to
// services/checkout-web/. A UI is a first-class C4 system: it has containers, it
// has runtime dependencies, and its pages (ui/pages/*.page.yaml) hang off the
// operations the edges below name.
//
// An EXTENDING model: no `specification` block, no partner copies. The kinds and
// every element this file points at — `customer`, `marketplace.orderService`,
// `marketplace.paymentService` — are declared once in
// architecture/landscape.likec4, and the root `likec4.config.json` does not
// exclude `services/**`, so the map and every model are read as ONE LikeC4
// project. `extend` takes the fully-qualified id, because the element is nested
// in the `marketplace` group.
//
// Note what this file does NOT do: it does not declare `customer = person`. The
// actor is the map's, and the edge below simply points at it. That is the rule
// the shape turns on — an element declared here outside
// `marketplace.checkoutWeb` is `c4.element-unowned` — and it is also what stops
// two documents from holding two different descriptions of the same person.
// SCHEMA.md, "Two shapes of a service model", has the other shape.
model {
  extend marketplace.checkoutWeb {
    spa = container 'spa' {
      description 'Checkout SPA — cart, payment and order status pages'
      technology 'React'
    }
    // A backend-for-frontend, drawn because it is where the fleet call actually
    // originates: the browser holds a session cookie, the fleet speaks bearer
    // tokens, and something has to be the place that translation happens. Drawn
    // as a container of checkout-web and not as a service of its own, because it
    // is deployed and released with the SPA and has no consumer but the SPA —
    // a services/checkout-bff/ would owe a spec, a contract and an owner nobody has.
    bff = container 'bff' {
      description 'Backend-for-frontend: session to bearer, response shaping'
      technology 'Node.js'
    }

    spa -> bff 'Calls the checkout BFF'
  }

  // The person is the MAP's element and the hop into this UI is drawn from it.
  // The landscape draws the coarser `customer -> marketplace.checkoutWeb` twin
  // at fleet scale; this one says which container the click reaches. A person is
  // exempt from the service/directory cross-check either way.
  customer -> marketplace.checkoutWeb.spa 'Buys'

  // The services this one actually calls, drawn with the edge the service really
  // has — the landscape carries only the boundary crossing (checkout-web ->
  // order-service), while the edges below say WHICH container makes the call.
  // Without them this file would be two boxes and a hop, and `c4.no-relationships`
  // would be right to say a model that reaches nothing is almost never true.
  //
  // `metadata { op }` is the C4 half of the operation join: the exact
  // operationId order-service's openapi.yaml declares. What `loam validate`
  // grades is the LANDSCAPE's twin of each edge below — `spine.op-undefined`
  // (error) is what it says there when the id names no operation the callee
  // defines. Carrying the op here anyway is what keeps the two documents from
  // drifting into different answers about which operation this container calls;
  // nothing would catch it if they did. Two calls to one service are two edges,
  // not one edge with a list — a view has to be able to show that the status
  // page and the checkout page stand on different operations.
  marketplace.checkoutWeb.bff -> marketplace.orderService 'Calls createOrder' {
    metadata {
      op 'createOrder'
    }
  }
  marketplace.checkoutWeb.bff -> marketplace.orderService 'Calls getOrder' {
    metadata {
      op 'getOrder'
    }
  }
  // Card details go from the browser straight to payment-service and never
  // through the BFF or order-service, which is why this edge starts at the SPA.
  // It carries no `metadata { op }` deliberately: its twin in the landscape is
  // the example's demonstration of `spine.op-link-missing` (warn) — the finding
  // for an edge that SAYS it calls something and names no operation, so nothing
  // can check the call still exists. Free here and one warning there, which is
  // the asymmetry worth knowing: the fleet map is what the spine reads.
  marketplace.checkoutWeb.spa -> marketplace.paymentService 'Calls the payment API'
}

// Views are the LikeC4 renderer's, never loam's — no check reads this block.
// Scoped to the service's element so the page answers "what does the checkout UI
// stand on" rather than redrawing the fleet, which architecture/landscape.likec4
// already does. This file is part of the root project: `npx likec4 start` at the
// docs root draws it, with no per-service project file to write.
views {
  view checkout_web of marketplace.checkoutWeb {
    title 'checkout-web — containers and the services they call'
    include *
    include marketplace.orderService, marketplace.paymentService
  }
}
